  m_Name: ReactorCoolingPumpData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Reactor Cooling Pump
  engineDescription: A Reactor Cooling Pump is a specialized pump used to continuously
    circulate coolant through a nuclear reactor system to remove heat generated by
    the reactor core. It operates by maintaining the required coolant flow and pressure
    throughout the reactor cooling circuit. Its major components include the pump
    casing, impeller, shaft, motor, bearings, mechanical seals, inlet and outlet
    connections, and monitoring systems. During operation, the pump circulates coolant
    through the reactor core, where the coolant absorbs heat produced by nuclear
    fission. The heated coolant then transfers this thermal energy to a steam generator
    or other heat-removal system before returning to the reactor. Reactor cooling
    pumps are essential components of nuclear power plants and other reactor cooling
    systems, where continuous heat removal is critical. From an engineering perspective,
    coolant flow rate, pump head, pressure, temperature, cavitation, sealing, vibration,
    material compatibility, redundancy, and emergency cooling capability are important
    considerations for safe and reliable operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
